/-
# The variety generated by the block lattices of groups (Kourovka Notebook Problem 9.62)

Kourovka Notebook, Archive of Solved Problems, Problem 9.62 (D. M. Smirnov): in any group `G`, the
cosets of all of its normal subgroups together with the empty set form the block lattice `C(G)` with
respect to inclusion, which is subdirectly irreducible for infinite `|G|` and simple for finite `|G|
≥ 3`. How large is the class of such lattices? Is every finite lattice embeddable in the lattice
`C(G)` for some finite group `G`?
-/

module

public import Batteries.Util.ProofWanted
public import WantedExt.GroupTheory.BlockLatticeVarietyKourovka11Problem93Wanted

@[expose] public section

namespace MathlibExt.GroupTheory.BlockLatticeVarietyFiniteLatticesKourovka9Problem62Wanted

/-!
Source: E. I. Khukhro and V. D. Mazurov (eds.), Unsolved Problems in Group Theory: The Kourovka
Notebook, 21st issue, arXiv:1401.0300v49 (2026), Archive of Solved Problems, Problem 9.62 (D. M.
Smirnov). UnsolvedMath record UM-00700595 (`KOU-9.62`), dataset ulamai/UnsolvedMath v1.8.0.

The Archive answers with two results of Smirnov on the class of block lattices: the filters `{x ∈
C(G) | g ∈ x}` are modular (even arguesian), and the variety generated by the block lattices of
groups does not contain all finite lattices. The second result is formalized, as the size of the
class asked about in the first question: by Birkhoff's theorem the variety generated by a class
contains a lattice iff the lattice satisfies every lattice identity of the class, so the statement
is that some lattice identity holding in the block lattice of every group fails in some finite
lattice. Blocks and lattice terms evaluated in `C(G)` (meets are intersections, joins are
`blockSup`) are `IsBlock` and `LatticeTerm.evalBlock` from the 11.93 entry, whose statement uses the
same result for the block lattices of finite groups and all lattices. The embedding question is
`finiteLatticeEmbedsInBlockLattice`: an embedding of a finite lattice `L` in `C(G)` is an injective
map from `L` to the blocks of `G` that sends meets to intersections and joins to `blockSup`. The
Archive does not state its answer separately, but the answer No follows from the variety statement:
a lattice identity that holds in `C(G)` holds in every lattice embedded in `C(G)`, so if every
finite lattice embedded in the block lattice of a finite group, the variety generated by the block
lattices of groups would contain every finite lattice.
-/

open MathlibExt.GroupTheory.BlockLatticeVarietyKourovka11Problem93Wanted

/-- Kourovka 9.62, first question (D. M. Smirnov), in the form of the Archive's answer: the variety
generated by the block lattices `C(G)` of all groups contains every finite lattice, i.e. every
lattice identity `p = q` that holds in the block lattice of every group (blocks `IsBlock` and term
values `LatticeTerm.evalBlock`, from the 11.93 entry) holds in every finite lattice. -/
def blockLatticeVarietyContainsFiniteLattices : Prop :=
  ∀ p q : LatticeTerm,
    (∀ (G : Type) [Group G] (v : ℕ → Set G), (∀ n, IsBlock (v n)) →
      p.evalBlock v = q.evalBlock v) →
    ∀ (L : Type) [Lattice L] [Finite L] (v : ℕ → L), p.eval v = q.eval v

/--
Resolved false: the Kourovka Archive answers 9.62 with Smirnov's result that the variety generated
by the block lattices of groups does not contain all finite lattices: some lattice identity that
holds in the block lattice `C(G)` of every group fails in some finite lattice. Source: E. I. Khukhro
and V. D. Mazurov (eds.), Unsolved Problems in Group Theory: The Kourovka Notebook, 21st issue,
arXiv:1401.0300v49 (2026), Archive of Solved Problems, Problem 9.62 (D. M. Smirnov); resolution: D.
M. Smirnov, On embedding lattices into block lattices of regular algebras and groups, Siberian Math.
J. 33 (1992), no. 4, 663-668, Kourovka Archive of Solved Problems, Problem 9.62: for each g ∈ G the
filter {x ∈ C(G) | g ∈ x} is modular (even arguesian); the variety generated by the block lattices
of groups does not contain all finite lattices, https://doi.org/10.1007/BF00971131.
-/
public theorem_wanted blockLatticeVarietyContainsFiniteLattices_refuted :
    ¬ blockLatticeVarietyContainsFiniteLattices

/-- Kourovka 9.62, second question (D. M. Smirnov): every finite lattice `L` embeds in the block
lattice `C(G)` of some finite group `G`, i.e. some injective map `f` sends the elements of `L` to
blocks of `G` (`IsBlock`), meets to intersections and joins to `blockSup`. -/
def finiteLatticeEmbedsInBlockLattice : Prop :=
  ∀ (L : Type) [Lattice L] [Finite L],
    ∃ (G : Type) (_ : Group G) (_ : Finite G) (f : L → Set G), Function.Injective f ∧
      (∀ x, IsBlock (f x)) ∧ (∀ x y, f (x ⊓ y) = f x ∩ f y) ∧
        ∀ x y, f (x ⊔ y) = blockSup (f x) (f y)

/--
Resolved false: the answer to the second question of 9.62 is No. It follows from Smirnov's result
recorded in the Kourovka Archive, that the variety generated by the block lattices of groups does
not contain all finite lattices: if every finite lattice `L` embedded in the block lattice `C(G)` of
a finite group `G`, every lattice identity that holds in the block lattice of every group would hold
in `L`, because an embedding carries the values of lattice terms in `L` to their values in `C(G)`.
Source: E. I. Khukhro and V. D. Mazurov (eds.), Unsolved Problems in Group Theory: The Kourovka
Notebook, 21st issue, arXiv:1401.0300v49 (2026), Archive of Solved Problems, Problem 9.62 (D. M.
Smirnov), which cites D. M. Smirnov, Siberian Math. J. 33 (1992), no. 4, 663-668, for that result.
-/
public theorem_wanted finiteLatticeEmbedsInBlockLattice_refuted :
    ¬ finiteLatticeEmbedsInBlockLattice

end MathlibExt.GroupTheory.BlockLatticeVarietyFiniteLatticesKourovka9Problem62Wanted
